uses:
  - mayo
notes: |
  Two routes. The quick one stacks garlic onto finished mayo; the scratch one builds it in
  from the yolk with part olive oil, which is the better sauce if you're making it anyway.

  Note that mayo already carries a grated clove per 16g yolk, so the quick route is adding
  garlic to garlic — that's the point, but taste before you add the second clove.

  Don't build it on 100% extra virgin. High-speed blending shears EVOO and turns it bitter
  and metallic; cut it with a neutral oil and you get the flavour without the harshness.

  Raw garlic sharpens as it sits — it's mellowest in the first hour and aggressive by the
  next day. Make it close to serving. Microplaned goes creamier and more pungent than
  crushed, and won't leave you biting into a chunk.

  No basis set on purpose — the two routes are alternatives, so scaling one off the other
  would be nonsense. Scale by eye.
components:
  - name: quick (from mayo)
    ingredients:
      - item: mayo
        mass: 100
      - item: garlic
        qty: 2
        unit: clove
        note: microplaned to a paste
      - item: lemon juice
        mass: 8
      - item: extra virgin olive oil
        mass: 10
        note: stirred in at the end, not blended
      - item: salt
        note: to taste
    steps:
      - paste the garlic with a pinch of salt under the flat of a knife, or microplane it
      - stir through the mayo with the lemon juice
      - fold in the olive oil last, by hand
      - rest 15 mins before tasting — the garlic needs a moment to come out
  - name: from scratch
    ingredients:
      - item: egg yolk
        mass: 16
        qty: 1
        unit: ea
      - item: garlic
        mass: 12
        qty: 4
        unit: clove
        note: microplaned
      - item: neutral oil
        mass: 120
      - item: extra virgin olive oil
        mass: 40
      - item: lemon juice
        mass: 12
      - item: dijon mustard
        mass: 5
      - item: salt
        mass: 2
    steps:
      - egg yolk, garlic, salt and dijon in the bottom of a tall jug or carafe
      - immersion blender down on the yolk, start blending before adding anything
      - slowly incorporate the neutral oil until it takes
      - lemon juice in, then the olive oil last and slowest
      - if it splits, start a fresh yolk in a clean jug and blend the broken sauce into it as if it were the oil
  - name: traditional (no egg)
    ingredients:
      - item: garlic
        qty: 4
        unit: clove
      - item: salt
        note: coarse, as the abrasive
      - item: extra virgin olive oil
        note: added drop by drop
    steps:
      - pound garlic and coarse salt to a smooth paste in a mortar
      - work in the olive oil drop by drop, grinding constantly, until it thickens and holds
    notes: |
      The actual Provençal thing — garlic, salt, oil, no egg, emulsified by the garlic's own
      pectin and a lot of patience. Fragile and much sharper than the mayo-based versions.
